implementationPlan.description:
Restore narrowly bounded Codex construction-site extraction, tests first. This is a two-file daemon contract repair, not a launcher change. Before editing, confirm task-7db14e2df19949a8bfdb150e0f29ffcb has landed from Moe sibling landing evidence or its Moe-Task commit trailer; read its committed scripts/moe-agent.sh and scripts/moe-agent.ps1, not a peer's uncommitted launcher changes. Keep the dependency. Note foreign dirty paths and leave them alone.

The existing 23 tests are the regression: an architect's complete archive of 76ea863361b9e1ecbb4f53dd827112377cbf6e6a produced 4 failed / 19 passed (site floor, shared facts, emitter facts, divergent facts). Preserve every original assertion verbatim in strength, including site >200, MAX_BRANCH_LINES=400, six global exceptions, all fact counts, emitter minimum length/count, whole-other-launcher absence, and four distinct whole launch invocations. Do not add global exceptions, widen matching to the whole wrapper, or skip tests.

In providerDescriptor.contract.test.ts, keep BRANCH_ANCHORS' existing provider-branch extraction and add only the two named config writers to site. Reuse the existing line split, comment stripping and Bash continuation normalization. Suggested definitions:
```ts
const WRITER_ANCHORS: Record<WrapperId, RegExp> = {
  bash: /^write_codex_config\(\) \{$/,
  powershell: /^function Write-MoeCodexConfig \{$/,
};
// Make source injection possible for local extractor assertions:
function buildView(id: WrapperId, raw = loadWrapper(id)): WrapperView
```
For the selected writer, require exactly one raw-line start match. Collect through its unindented closing line ('}'), inclusive; match RAW lines, never trim(), because PowerShell nested blocks close with indented braces. Fail with a wrapper/function-specific error on absent/duplicate start, EOF, a new top-level function before the close, or a span exceeding MAX_BRANCH_LINES. No fallback to EOF/whole-file matching. Track writer span separately from branchSpans, append this one bounded body to siteLines, and apply the existing normalization. This is error handling for malformed anchors, not a shell parser; comment the top-level-brace formatting assumption.

Extend the existing fixture-anchor and sentinel test bodies rather than replacing or removing their assertions (retain 23 test cases): pin one nonempty bounded writer per wrapper; inject LF and CRLF versions and assert identical normalized sites; assert missing writer, duplicate writer, missing terminator/next top-level function, and an oversized writer throw; put a distinctive executable-text sentinel just outside the writer and in an unrelated provider/helper and assert it is absent from site. The real writer includes nested blocks and its later emitters, so those must remain captured. Keep existing missing-file diagnostics. Update only this test file's scope comments/diagnostics to say Codex branches plus named writer; no docs sweep or new extraction dependency.
